|~ `::proj`, the step that reads a part of a value, and what is declared
   under it: its constructor and the refusals of a read that misses. ~|

:proj
  |~~ The step of a projection, `/a/0/b`: it reads a part of the value in
      the pipe by keys and indices, a negative index from the end, and a
      miss answers an error, never null, so a misspelled field is found
      where it is read. `/` is the value itself. A key is written as a
      keyword is, bare, in quotes, `/"foo bar"`, or with its colon when it
      is a path, `/:qlang/error`, which is one key where `/qlang/error`
      is two. As a record, `:path` is the keys and indices it walks,
      `[]` for `/`.
      ::snippet~({:a {:b [5 6]}} | /a/b/-1) answers `6`.
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/holdsThePath
  |~~ A projection holds its path of keys and indices. ~~|
  ::law~(~(/items/0/name) | first | /path | eq [:items 0 :name])

:proj/holdsTheEmptyPath
  |~~ `/` alone has the empty path. ~~|
  ::law~(~(/) | first | /path | eq [])

:proj/readsQuotedAndPathKeys
  |~~ Quoted keys and keys with a path read in a path. ~~|
  ::law~({:"foo bar" {:qlang/error 1}} | /"foo bar"/:qlang/error | eq 1)

:proj/readsNestedKeys
  |~~ A path of two keys reads nested maps. ~~|
  ::law~({:qlang {:error 2}} | /qlang/error | eq 2)

:proj/readsTheWholeValue
  |~~ `/` reads the whole value. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | mul / | eq 25)

:proj/construct
  |~~ The step of a projection from its record, `:path`, which reads
      back as itself.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:proj/PayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A `::proj` is a map of `:path`, a vector of keys and integers, and
      no string: `{:path [:items 0]} | tag ::proj` is the step
      `/items/0`. `:field` names the field that does not fit.
      ::link~(::proj | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesAPathThatIsAString
  |~~ A path that is a string is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
  ::law~(::proj{:path "items"} !| [type /field] | eq [::proj/PayloadNotSchemaError :path])

:proj/PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheStepOfAPath
  |~~ A path of keys and indices builds the step that walks it. ~~|
  ::law~({:path [:items 0]} | tag ::proj | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(/items/0))

:proj/ReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The `::proj` prints as text that reads back as another step: a key
      that reads as an index, `:"0"`, prints as `/"0"`, which reads back
      as the index `0`; `:printed` is that text. The path holds the index,
      `{:path [0]}`, which reads the key `"0"` of a map as well,
      `{:"0" 5} | /0` answering `5`.
      ::link~(::proj | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesAKeyThatReadsAsAnIndex
  |~~ A key that reads as an index prints as the index. ~~|
  ::law~(::proj{:path [:"0"]} !| [type /printed] | eq [::proj/ReadBackDiffersError "/\"0\""])

:proj/ReadBackDiffersError/readsTheKeyOfAMapByTheIndex
  |~~ The index reads the key of a map that spells it. ~~|
  ::law~({:"0" 5} | /0 | eq 5)

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError
  |~~ An index reads within a vector or a set, from `/0` to the last,
      `/-1`, a negative index counting from the end; `:index` holds the
      index read and `:length` the length of the subject. The last
      element is `/-1` whatever the length, an index that may lie past
      the end is read by `at`, which answers null there, and `take` and
      `drop` answer the part there is without refusing.
      ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::vec/at | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError/index
  |~~ The index the projection read, counted from the end when negative. ~~|
  ::number

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError/length
  |~~ How many elements the value holds. ~~|
  ::number

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAnIndexPastTheEnd
  |~~ An index past the end is refused with the length. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | /99 !| [type /index /length] | eq [::proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError 99 3])

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError/refusesAnIndexBeforeTheStart
  |~~ A negative index past the start is refused as it was written. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | /-4 !| [type /index /length] | eq [::proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError -4 3])

:proj/IndexOutOfBoundsError/readsTheLastFromTheEnd
  |~~ `/-1` reads the last element whatever the length. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | /-1 | eq 30)

:proj/KeyNotInMapError
  |~~ A projection names a key the map holds, so a misspelled field is
      found where it is read; `:key` holds the key read, and `:nearest`
      the keys of the map it was likely meant to be, near it in spelling
      as a name is near the names of the scope. `keys` lists the keys
      there are, `{:a 1} | keys`, and a key that may be absent is read by
      `at`, which answers null for it, a `coalesce` after it giving the
      fallback, `at :b | coalesce 0`.
      ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::map/at | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/KeyNotInMapError/key
  |~~ The key the projection read, which the map does not hold. ~~|
  ::keyword

:proj/KeyNotInMapError/refusesAKeyTheMapLacks
  |~~ A key the map lacks is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | /b !| [type /key] | eq [::proj/KeyNotInMapError :b])

:proj/KeyNotInMapError/suggestsTheNearestKey
  |~~ The keys of the map nearest in spelling are suggested. ~~|
  ::law~({:emails [] :age 3} | /email !| /nearest | eq [:emails])

:proj/SubjectNotProjectableError
  |~~ A projection reads a part of a map, a vector or a set, one under a
      tag among them; a number, a string or null has no parts, and `:key`
      holds the key or the index read. A modifier reads the subject of
      its own step: after `/qty |` the subject is the number, so two
      fields of one record are read in one step by a form whose args they
      are, `mulOf /qty /price`. A value that may be null is read by `at`,
      which answers null.
      ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/SubjectNotProjectableError/key
  |~~ The segment the projection read, a key or an index. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::number]

:proj/SubjectNotProjectableError/readsTwoFieldsInOneStep
  |~~ Two fields of one record combine in one step by `mulOf`. ~~|
  ::law~({:qty 2 :price 3} | mulOf /qty /price | eq 6)

:proj/SubjectNotProjectableError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no fields. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | /foo
    !| [type /key /actualType] | eq [::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError :foo ::number])

:proj/SubjectNotProjectableError/refusesNull
  |~~ Null has no parts. ~~|
  ::law~(null | /k !| [type /actualType] | eq [::proj/SubjectNotProjectableError ::null])

:proj/IndexNotIntegerError
  |~~ A vector is read by an index, `/0`, and a set by the index of an
      element in its one order; `:key` holds the field read. A field of
      each element is read by `map`, `map ~/name`, and the elements a field
      picks are found by `filter`, `filter ~(/n | gt 1)`.
      ::link~(::proj | doc) ::link~(::vec/filter | doc)
  ~~|

:proj/IndexNotIntegerError/key
  |~~ The segment the projection read, which names no index. ~~|
  ::keyword

:proj/IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfAVector
  |~~ A vector is read by an index, not a field. ~~|
  ::law~([10 20 30] | /name !| [type /key] | eq [::proj/IndexNotIntegerError :name])

:proj/IndexNotIntegerError/refusesAFieldOfASet
  |~~ A set is read by an index, not a field. ~~|
  ::law~(#[:a :b :c] | /name !| [type /key] | eq [::proj/IndexNotIntegerError :name])

:proj/IndexNotIntegerError/readsAFieldOfEachElement
  |~~ A field of each element is read by `map`. ~~|
  ::law~([{:name "a"} {:name "b"}] | map ~/name | eq ["a" "b"])
